<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Grijalva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1490–<a href="/years/1527">1527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de Grijalva was a Spanish conquistador who led the second expedition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1518. He sailed past Cozumel, fought at Champotón and followed the Gulf coast to the river now named after him, gathering the first reports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. His voyage prepared the way for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> in 1527.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva.jpg" alt="Juan de Grijalva" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_de_Grijalva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juan de Grijalva was born around 1490 and was a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Diego Velázquez</a>. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1508 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1511. In 1518 he became one of the first to explore the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> put the numbers of the 1518 expedition at 170 people, while Pedro Mártir gave 300. The main pilot was Antón de Alaminos, and the party included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a>. Two natives of Yucatan, Julianillo and Melchorejo, who had been captured during the Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> expedition, served as interpreters. The four ships left the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> on 8 April 1518.</p><p>After rounding the Guaniguanico in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, Grijalva sailed along the Mexican coast, reached Cozumel on 3 May 1518 and arrived on 8 June at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> region in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The Río Grijalva was named after him. He was the first Spaniard to meet a delegation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. One of the natives who joined the expedition was baptised Francisco and later served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> interpreter for Cortés. According to W. H. Prescott's Conquest of Mexico, Grijalva was the first Spaniard to bring back word of the gold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, which spurred the drive for conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a> wrote about the voyage in his Historia.</p><p>In 1518 Cortés stayed at Grijalva's home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, at the start of his own Mexican expedition, and recruited men there, including the five Alvarado brothers. Grijalva was killed by natives in Honduras on 21 January 1527.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan de Grijalva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Grijalva</b>'s expedition fought there again in 1518.</blockquote><a href="/state/champoton">Champotón</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Grijalva</b> landed in 1518 and Cortés in 1519, smashing idols and recovering the castaway Jerónimo de Aguilar.</blockquote><a href="/state/cozumel">Cozumel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cuéllar, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Olancho Department, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.4,-4.31&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Cuéllar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4445 688 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan de Grijalva"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4895 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4895 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.4,-4.31&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
